import json,re
c=json.load(open('cur.json'));T=c['T'];idx=c['idx']
def u(s): return len(s.encode('utf-16-le'))//2
a=T.index('To compare the model with the recordings, we converted'); b=T.index('(Supplementary Fig. 1i).',a)+24
v0=T.index('To validate this read-out, we drove',a)
valid=T[v0:b].replace('To validate this read-out, we drove','We drove',1)
meth=("To compare the model with the recordings, On and Off states had to be measured in the model exactly as in the data. In the recordings, they are defined on the z score of a decoder trained on a finite sample of neurons, not on the activity of the network itself, and this z score is noisy even when the memory is fully present. Labelling the pool rates of the model directly would ignore this read-out noise, and with it the dependence of On/Off statistics on how well the recorded neurons read out the memory (Fig. 2). We therefore converted the model's activity into a decoding z score with the same time resolution, noise and labelling as the data (Supplementary Fig. 1).\n"
"The model has no neurons, so the decoding evidence was Δr(t), the difference between the cued and uncued pool rates. Δr is large when the cued pool is active and near zero when both pools are silent, and it was averaged in 100 ms windows stepped by 10 ms, as the neural data were. The model z score was z(t) = c Δr(t) + η(t), where the gain c converts rates into z units and η is read-out noise, which stands for the variability of a decoder trained on a finite number of neurons and trials.\n"
"The noise was constrained by the data before the cue, where the decoder z carries no memory signal and therefore reflects read-out noise alone. Like the pre-cue z, η has unit variance (the SD of pre-cue z was 1.06) and the autocorrelation expected from overlapping 100 ms windows. In addition, a constant per-trial offset carries a share ρ of its variance, equal to the autocorrelation of the data's pre-cue z at 200 ms, beyond the overlap of the windows (0.35 for all neurons, 0.09 for the most selective neurons and 0.11 for random neurons; Supplementary Fig. 1c). This offset captures slow fluctuations of the decoder output from trial to trial, which are largest for decoders using all neurons.\n"
"The gain c was not fitted to the On/Off statistics, so that these statistics are not built into the read-out. Instead, it was calibrated on a separate property of the data, the level that z reaches when the memory is read out, which we call the decoding strength. The mean of the delay z would not serve this purpose, because the delay z mixes time points with strong and weak decoding, and its mean therefore depends on how much of the delay is spent Off, which is what the model is fitted to and tested against. We therefore fitted a two-component Gaussian mixture to the delay z values (500–1,400 ms after cue onset) and took the mean of the upper component, which reflects the level of z during strong decoding and depends much less on how often z is low. For each decoded population, c was set so that the decoding strength of the model's delay z equalled the data's, averaged over sessions (Supplementary Fig. 1d,e). Decoding strength increases steeply and monotonically with c, so c is well determined.\n"
"States were labelled exactly as in Panichello et al. (2024), so that model and data are compared with identical definitions. On states are runs of z > 1.96 whose summed z exceeds the 95th percentile of the same quantity in pure noise η. Off states are runs of at least 5 points (50 ms) with z < 0.84. States were counted from 0 to 1,400 ms after cue onset, and fractions of the delay spent On or Off from 500 to 1,400 ms. Model statistics were computed from 2,112 simulated trials (792 per evaluation during fitting). Data statistics are means over the 25 sessions; for random neurons, the 10 draws were first averaged within each session.\n"
"Because this read-out is a simplification of a real decoder, we validated it on synthetic data in which the true dips are known. "+valid)
cap=("Supplementary Figure 1. Reading the model out like the data. a, One model trial through the read-out. From top to bottom, rates of the cued (black) and uncued (grey) pools, with dips of the common input in grey; Δr, their difference averaged in 100 ms windows every 10 ms; c·Δr (black) and the read-out noise η (grey); and z = c·Δr + η, with On (red) and Off (cyan) states labelled with the thresholds of Panichello et al. (2024) (dashed, z = 1.96 and 0.84). "
"b, One data trial (all-unit decoder, session 211008) with its On and Off states. "
"c, Autocorrelation of the decoder z before the cue, where there is no memory signal, for three decoded populations (black and greys), compared with the model's noise η (blue dashed) and with η plus 4% white noise (purple dashed). "
"d, Delay z (500–1,400 ms after cue onset) of one example session (grey; session 240209, whose decoding strength is closest to the mean over sessions), with the two components of a Gaussian mixture fitted to it (dotted, lower; solid, upper), and the model's delay z (blue). Dashed, decoding strength, the mean of the upper component (2.22 for this session, 2.18 averaged over sessions, 2.20 for the model). "
"e, Decoding strength of the model as a function of the gain c. The data's decoding strength (horizontal line) sets c (vertical line). "
"f–h, Validation on synthetic data. The fitted model drove 330 Poisson neurons whose spikes were decoded with the data pipeline. f, Example trial with the true dips (grey), the decoder's z (black) and the read-out z = c·Δr + η (blue). g, Correlation of each read-out's delay z with the decoder's, at three decoding strengths matched to the data's random-20, all-unit and top-20 decoders. Grey, the decoder rerun on new spikes, which sets the ceiling for two noisy read-outs. h, On and Off statistics of the decoder (black), the read-out (blue) and the read-out with jitter-matched noise (purple), mean ± 2 SEM over 8 synthetic sessions. "
"i, Controls on the fitted model with the all-unit read-out. Off (cyan) and On (red) states per trial, and percentage of the delay Off, for the data, the read-out used in the paper, noise only (c = 0), gain ×0.75 and ×1.25, ρ = 0 and 0.7, jitter-matched noise and the model without dips.")
k=T.index('Supplementary Figure 1. Reading'); e=T.index('\n',k)
E=[(idx[k],idx[e-1]+1,cap),(idx[a],idx[b-1]+1,meth)]
R=[]
for s,e_,n in sorted(E,key=lambda x:-x[0]):
    R+=[{"deleteContentRange":{"range":{"startIndex":s,"endIndex":e_}}},{"insertText":{"location":{"index":s},"text":n}}]
    for m in re.finditer(r'\b(?:On|Off)(?:/(?:On|Off))?\b',n):
        p=s+u(n[:m.start()]); R.append({"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":p,"endIndex":p+u(m.group(0))},"textStyle":{"italic":True},"fields":"italic"}})
json.dump(R,open('req_meth.json','w'))
print(len(R)); print(json.dumps(R))
